% The keys and hashes of the policy, as the source writes them.
the public key of cosigner A is "03a0434d9e47f3c86235477c7b1ae6ae5d3442d49b1943c2b752a68e2a47e247c7".

the public key of cosigner B is "03774ae7f858a9411e5ef4246b70c65aac5649980be5c17891bbec17895da008cb".

the public key of cosigner C is "03d01115d548e7561b15c38f004d734633687cf4419620095bc5b0f47070afe85a".

% The spending paths of the policy.
rule two_of_three with provenance the Bitcoin Core descriptor documentation,
        confer "describes a P2WSH *2-of-3* multisig output with keys in the specified order":
the coin can be spent if
    a number N is the count of each K such that
        a key K is in [cosigner A, cosigner B, cosigner C]
        and the witness contains a signature by K
    and N >= 2.

scenario two_of_three_1 is, as stated in the satisfaction analysis of the policy:
    % spending path two_of_three_1 — the path needs: a signature by cosigner B, a signature by cosigner A
    the witness contains a signature by cosigner B.
    the witness contains a signature by cosigner A.
    spendable expects answers ["the coin can be spent"].

scenario two_of_three_1_without_1 is, as stated in the satisfaction analysis of the policy:
    % spending path two_of_three_1 without requirement 1 — the path needs: a signature by cosigner B, a signature by cosigner A
    the witness contains a signature by cosigner A.
    spendable expects answers [].

